Z||000010||CHUCKVH||11-27-2001||01:14 AM||||It's not just the KYB struts!  I just installed Monroe Sensatrac gas struts on my 88 coupe, and I found the same interference problem with the right strut/knuckle assembly.  Not only that, but upon inspection, the factory original strut that I removed from the right side showed where the knuckle had been in contact with the strut in the exact same location.  No signs of contact with the left knuckle on either the original strut or the replacement.  It appears that some right hand knuckle forgings have an "extra meaty" ridge of  material inboard of and below the centerline of the upper bolt hole.  I rounded and polished this ridge with my makita 4 inch sander, and eliminated all contact without reducing the cross section of the knuckle.  One other item of note:  It is possible that due to manufacturing tolerances, some 88's may not be able to achieve the correct camber settings using the stock bolts.  This is because the lower bolt will not travel to the end of the elongated slot, due to the built in washer hitting the lip of the strut bracket.  Install the bolt, nut and washer onto the strut before you install it, and try to get the shank of the bolt to reach the end of the slot.  You will see what I mean.<P>Chuck ||||reg||1
